tation></ref><ref id="B27"><label>27</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Messa</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Choi</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoh</surname><given-names>CK</given-names></name><name><surname>Jacobs</surname><given-names>EL</given-names></name><name><surname>Glaspy</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Rege</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Quantification of glucose utilization in liver metastases: parametric imaging of FDG uptake with PET</article-title><source>J Comput Assist Tomogr</source><year>1992</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>684</fpage><lpage>689</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1522257</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B28"><label>28</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nagata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamamoto</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Hiraoka</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Abe</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Takahashi</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Akuta</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Monitoring liver tumor therapy with [18F]FDG positron emission tomography</article-title><source>J Comput Assist Tomogr</source><year>1990</year><volume>14</volume><fpage>370</fpage><lpage>374</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2335602</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B29"><label>29</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Okazumi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Isono</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Enomoto</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Kikuchi</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Ozaki</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamamoto</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Evaluation of liver tumors using fluorine-18-fluorodeoxyglucose PET: characterization of tumor and assessment of effect of treatment</article-title><source>J Nucl Med</source><year>1992</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>333</fpage><lpage>339</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1311035</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B30"><label>30</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Torizuka</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamaki</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Inokuma</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Magata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Yonekura</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Tanaka</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Value of fluorine-18-FDG-PET to monitor hepatocellular carcinoma after interventional therapy</article-title><source>J Nucl Med</source><year>1994</year><volume>35</volume><fpage>1965</fpage><lpage>1969</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7989978</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B31"><label>31</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oya</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Nagata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Tamaki</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Takagi</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Murata</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Magata</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>FDG-PET evaluation of therapeutic effects on VX2 liver tumor</article-title><source>J Nucl Med</source><year>1996</year><volume>37</volume><fpage>296</fpage><lpage>302</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8667065</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B32"><label>32</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Spaepen</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Stroobants</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Dupont</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Bormans</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Balzarini</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Verhoef</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>[(18)F]FDG PET monitoring of tumour response to chemotherapy: does [(18)F]FDG uptake correlate with the viable tumour cell fraction?</article-title><source>Eur J Nucl Med Mol Imaging</source><year>2003</year><volume>30</volume><fpage>682</fpage><lpage>688</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12601498</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p><bold>A.</bold> Axial CT scan before treatment, the hepatic arterial phase (left) and the portal venous phase (right). Tumor is well demarcated in the left lobe of the liver. It shows irregular peripheral and septal enhancement and central low attenuation in the arterial phase (left, arrow). On the portal venous phase, arterial enhancement of the tumor washed out (right, arrow).</p><p><bold>B.</bold> Celiac arteriography shows hypervascular tumor staining in the left lobe of the liver (arrow).</p><p><bold>C.</bold> On the axial CT scan one week after treatment, the enhancing portion on the arterial and portal phases has almost decreased and larger area of low attenuation is seen as drug infusion defect (arrows).</p><p><bold>D.</bold> Gross specimen of the extracted liver shows massive necrosis of the tumor (arrows).</p><p><bold>E.</bold> Serial axial scans of FDG PET. Before treatment (left), the tumor is hypermetabolic with a SUV of 3.42 (arrow). It decreased to 2.00 immediately after treatment (middle, arrow), and it decreased more to 1.95 a week after the treatment (right, arrow).</p><p><bold>F.</bold> Hematoxylin-eosin staining of the cut surface of the specimen (&#x000d7; 1) revealed near total necrosis with a small proportion of residual viable tumor. The tumor necrosis rate was 99.00%.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-8-216-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>Line graph shows the chronological SUV changes of the VX2 liver tumors in each animal. Seven animals showed a gradually decreased SUV, but the other three animals showed rising tendency of FDG uptake at one week after treatment. <sup>*</sup> Tx = treatment.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-8-216-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>Scatterplots show correlation between SUV and the tumor necrosis rate (%) immediately (<bold>A</bold>) and one week (<bold>B</bold>) after treatment. No significant correlation between these parameters was observed (Spearman <italic>r</italic> = -0.29, <italic>p</italic> = 0.41 in <bold>A</bold>, Spearman <italic>r</italic> = 0.49, <italic>p</italic> = 0.89 in <bold>B</bold>).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-8-216-g003"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Chronological Changes of VX2 Liver Tumor after 3-BrPA Administration</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="kjr-8-216-i001"/><table-wrap-foot><fn><p>Nate.-HAP = hepatic arterial phase, PVP = portal venous phase, SUV = standardized uptake value, <sup>*</sup> high (or low) = when density of the tumor is higher (or lower) than that of background liver parenchyma. <sup>&#x02020;</sup>Rates of SUV decrease after treatment are given in the parentheses of each column of SUV.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>